#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100650 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39738d1a-2b58-58e7-8b93-fae76501045c" :knowledge-point/title "Minimizing the Surface Area of a Cylinder With a Fixed Volume"}] :question/id #uuid "c7f66eb5-8151-5886-9b35-58632319651f" :question/math-academy-id "e-351" :question/problem "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base but no lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $27\\pi\\, \\textrm{in}^3.$ What is the radius of the cylinder that gives the smallest surface area?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/ad/adb9c56132e8f346a07a9a644cd364c2347ad8c551a3c9bd4842b7067eb1d050.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS= 2\\pi r h + \\pi r^2.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $27\\pi \\, \\textrm{in}^3,$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n27\\pi = \\pi r^2 h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh = \\dfrac{27}{ r^2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nS(r)& = 2\\pi r \\left(\\dfrac{27}{ r^2}\\right) + \\pi r^2\\\n & = \\dfrac{54\\pi}{r} +  \\pi r^2.}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\nS'(r) = -\\dfrac{54\\pi}{r^2} + 2\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{54\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 2\\pi r & =\\frac{54\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 2\\pi {r}^{3} & =54\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\ r & =3\\,\\text{in}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r <   3$ then $S'(r) <         0,$ and if $r > 3$ then $S'(r) > 0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=3$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the smallest surface area has radius $r =3\\,\\textrm{in}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100651 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fae559-f88f-50ec-a1f0-a7cfeb319d19" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Volume of a Cylinder With a Fixed Surface Area"}] :question/id #uuid "c82b0d87-792b-57d2-9340-9095af643c44" :question/math-academy-id "e-352" :question/problem "We need to create a cylindrical tin with no lid using $25\\pi\\,\\mathrm{cm^2}$ of sheet metal. What is the radius $r$ of the cylinder that has the largest volume? \n\n![](images/a2/a2916167b3c8d4169339280fe67a0018b5d09a9784260cf6488cd6825753b2b9.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2 h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $25\\pi\\,\\mathrm{cm^2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi r h + \\pi r^2 = 25\\pi.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =25\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{25\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{25-{r}^{2}}{2r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\times \\frac{25-{r}^{2}}{2r} \\\\ V(r) & =\\frac{(25r-{r}^{3})\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n \n\n$$\nV'(r) = \\frac{(25 - 3 r^2)\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{(25-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2} & =0 \\\\ 25 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root in the last step because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r   = \\dfrac{5\\sqrt{3}}{3}$  is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\nV''(r) = -3\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that $V''\\left( \\dfrac{5\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right) < 0.$ So, the stationary point $r=\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\, \\textrm{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100783 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8ac7b46-da1d-57f9-ab61-efa672ca8128" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing the Cost of Building a Cylinder"}] :question/id #uuid "3d2657d6-3dc6-5855-8464-4f23dc675211" :question/math-academy-id "e-7523" :question/problem "A manufacturer wishes to build a cylindrical tank with a base and a lid. The tank must have an exact volume of $18\\pi\\,\\mathrm{m}^3.$\nTo make the tank, the manufacturer uses two materials:\n\n- a metal sheet that costs $\\[math]1.50$ per $\\mathrm{m}^2$ for the lateral surface,\n\n- a metal sheet that costs $\\[/math]0.50$ per $\\mathrm{m}^2$ for the base and lid.\n\nWhat is the radius of the tank that gives the smallest possible manufacturing cost?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/d7/d72b9a87e460f841bbd135fd16806607c16aa0b37b2279727bd7d9eeeb9c6982.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 1.5+2\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $18\\pi\\,\\textrm{m}^3,$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n18\\pi = \\pi r^2 h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constant equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh = \\dfrac{18}{r^2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =3\\pi r(\\frac{18}{{r}^{2}})+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{54\\pi }{r}+\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\nC'(r) = -\\dfrac{54\\pi}{r^2} + 2\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{54\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 2\\pi r & =\\frac{54\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 2\\pi {r}^{3} & =54\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\ r & =3\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r < 3$ then $C'(r) <    0,$ and if $r > 3$ then $C'(r) > 0,$ which means that the stationary point $r = 3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the tank with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r = 3\\,\\textrm{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39738d1a-2b58-58e7-8b93-fae76501045c" :knowledge-point/title "Minimizing the Surface Area of a Cylinder With a Fixed Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b4a6c19-602c-54ee-8152-81351ed0e7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509741} {:answer/id #uuid "c4704d4c-6962-5e51-9c39-966e80ebd0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510100} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cdea9b-fce1-5271-9532-93edde53a638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510134} {:answer/id #uuid "d7cff4ee-3f14-55d3-aab1-becaa9d5c30e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510146} {:answer/id #uuid "f62f9599-77a5-5414-840d-7de4098038df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f62f9599-77a5-5414-840d-7de4098038df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510214} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7e8ebb0-e3d6-56f0-bb35-2dd8e698b8c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c0fb2ee-81ac-5d75-ab41-5ee64f815cef" :question/math-academy-id "q-117348" :question/problem "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base and a lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $2\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ What is the radius of the cylinder that gives the smallest surface area?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/33/33c90b487d2b3036068acf4b771d2ca6ae95e8e8446b706b85fed058dc12a0bf.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $2\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n2\\,000\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{2\\,000}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{2\\,000}{{r}^{2}})+2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{r}+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{3} & =4\\,000\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =1\\,000 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{1\\,000} \\\\ r & =10\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<10$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>10$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=10$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the smallest surface area has radius $r=10\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fae559-f88f-50ec-a1f0-a7cfeb319d19" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Volume of a Cylinder With a Fixed Surface Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c7dbb34-59dd-5e81-a863-a5c9d70d0ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510014} {:answer/id #uuid "a417d5b7-1f44-5776-946d-4b89b7864438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510030} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c2e494-cb43-5ca0-8464-d42be445ccc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510042} {:answer/id #uuid "c5953b51-f0b1-5657-955a-863eedbda8b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510102} {:answer/id #uuid "c59ab5af-5a91-5ef6-bd69-64c38848927c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c59ab5af-5a91-5ef6-bd69-64c38848927c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510103} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9d445ab-f7a7-5bd0-ba53-a5e0545e4523" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f844afb-5020-5b9d-a059-ebc3dcd000de" :question/math-academy-id "q-117360" :question/problem "![](images/08/08f6351b8b95c87386d07466b6e876d72f33bc00034e83b360d03d23633d363d.png)\n\nWe need to create a cylindrical tin with a lid using $6\\pi \\,{ft}^{2}$ of sheet metal. What is the radius $r$ of the cylinder that has the largest volume?" :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $6\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =6\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{6\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{3-{r}^{2}}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}(\\frac{3-{r}^{2}}{r}) \\\\ V(r) & =(3r-{r}^{3})\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=(3-3{r}^{2})\\pi .\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ (3-3{r}^{2})\\pi  & =0 \\\\ 3 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =1 \\\\ r & =1\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=1$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=-6\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(1)<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=1$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=1\\,\\text{ft}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fae559-f88f-50ec-a1f0-a7cfeb319d19" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Volume of a Cylinder With a Fixed Surface Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e139e6d-04b2-50d6-80c2-c3eaf743883f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509851} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc53d03-6d9e-5293-ba20-93007fa4827a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509916} {:answer/id #uuid "735a2653-a0e7-558e-8ab6-95a5bb2ed37e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509929} {:answer/id #uuid "89e398d3-c766-5417-baa4-109f6c2e3d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509987} {:answer/id #uuid "d6da261c-3a8c-54a5-8d8c-d5b40017cbd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89e398d3-c766-5417-baa4-109f6c2e3d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509987} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f669200-1574-50fb-be9a-48d96ef7fd94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c69ae447-8c86-5d03-83d6-e7f16c525735" :question/math-academy-id "q-16231" :question/problem "![](images/86/8689a80bf3fddc055072d11837cd5bf2a6b4e966d935816fa1e8b68454774d24.png)\n\nWe need to create a cylindrical tin with no lid using $64\\pi \\,{cm}^{2}$ of a metal sheet. What is the radius $r$ of the cylinder that has the largest volume?" :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $64\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}=64\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =64\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{64\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{64-{r}^{2}}{2r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\times \\frac{64-{r}^{2}}{2r} \\\\ V(r) & =\\frac{(64r-{r}^{3})\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=\\frac{(64-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{(64-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2} & =0 \\\\ 64 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{64}{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=\\frac{-6\\pi r}{2}=-3\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3})<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fae559-f88f-50ec-a1f0-a7cfeb319d19" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Volume of a Cylinder With a Fixed Surface Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37d90ecc-799c-5966-9485-4403e472a148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\pi \\,{cm}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509809} {:answer/id #uuid "961c2b95-dc93-5b57-bf9b-e581d63cdc5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\pi \\,{cm}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510004} {:answer/id #uuid "9a21a290-1231-5251-ae15-843e314d1d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46\\pi \\,{cm}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510010} {:answer/id #uuid "9fcdc104-93f0-5be0-ac43-65beeb6f88d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\pi \\,{cm}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510022} {:answer/id #uuid "a0205964-c271-505f-ba22-0f2acac8f600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58\\pi \\,{cm}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37d90ecc-799c-5966-9485-4403e472a148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\pi \\,{cm}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509809} :answer-field/id #uuid "1be58fdc-55c3-5738-97fc-4b0fc03a148f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28541c18-bc9f-5800-8a64-d3d3ece27f6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-18444" :question/problem "![](images/50/50853fcaa44ff9b7d80e708e546275286541afc0b6ed05c4ca87ea3175364c09.png)\n\nWe need to create a cylindrical tin with no lid using $48\\pi \\,{cm}^{2}$ of sheet metal. What is the largest possible volume of the cylinder?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $48\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}=48\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =48\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{48\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{48-{r}^{2}}{2r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\times \\frac{48-{r}^{2}}{2r} \\\\ V(r) & =\\frac{(48r-{r}^{3})\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=\\frac{(48-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{(48-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2} & =0 \\\\ 48 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =16 \\\\ r & =4\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=4$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=\\frac{-6\\pi r}{2}=-3\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(4)<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=4$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nSo the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=4\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nFinally, to find the largest volume, we substitute $r=4\\,\\text{cm}$ into the equation for the volume $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(4) & =\\frac{(48(4)-(4)^{3})\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(192-64)\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =64\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the largest possible volume of the cylinder is $V=64\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fae559-f88f-50ec-a1f0-a7cfeb319d19" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Volume of a Cylinder With a Fixed Surface Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "355100b8-e40f-5a0c-b5ea-ca6b62af8d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509802} {:answer/id #uuid "5ef92129-ea31-5154-9c96-9f0c523bf5b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509888} {:answer/id #uuid "9f956854-f677-5cc1-9e81-5366dfda3bfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510021} {:answer/id #uuid "e1698f51-ddd7-52d0-a197-1eac20e0fb64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510167} {:answer/id #uuid "e892c93e-e0a8-5845-a04d-6f979c4c9224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ef92129-ea31-5154-9c96-9f0c523bf5b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509888} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c302848-acd9-5af2-94b7-b0a147f47676" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b58094e-e8c0-5c82-ac8b-027a5c669c62" :question/math-academy-id "q-18446" :question/problem "![](images/38/38883c7cc4bfe8c9ecccadfcc2d0c75ff66f79b19ff697c15ddbe53f4c63dbab.png)\n\nWe need to create a cylindrical tin with a lid using $216\\pi \\,{cm}^{2}$ of sheet metal. What is the radius $r$ of the cylinder that has the largest volume?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $216\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}=216\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =216\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{216\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{108-{r}^{2}}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\times \\frac{108-{r}^{2}}{r} \\\\ V(r) & =(108r-{r}^{3})\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=(108-3{r}^{2})\\pi .\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ (108-3{r}^{2})\\pi  & =0 \\\\ 108 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =36 \\\\ r & =6\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=6$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=-6\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(6)<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=6$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=6\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39738d1a-2b58-58e7-8b93-fae76501045c" :knowledge-point/title "Minimizing the Surface Area of a Cylinder With a Fixed Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1812698a-baa5-5c04-bbe5-0eaab253aef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48\\pi \\,{mm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509732} {:answer/id #uuid "39604b6a-d37e-5b93-b3dd-8a6382ccd526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\pi \\,{mm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509811} {:answer/id #uuid "3f3c1da7-84f5-5398-886d-21fd3f98354d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49\\pi \\,{mm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509823} {:answer/id #uuid "b4088f69-4254-5878-a7c1-d3dc65b31298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44\\pi \\,{mm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510075} {:answer/id #uuid "f9db4585-c2cb-517d-a138-24946d17f8e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45\\pi \\,{mm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1812698a-baa5-5c04-bbe5-0eaab253aef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48\\pi \\,{mm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509732} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae5cdce9-4bc0-5a63-9f3a-1ec87a9fb050" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1317ed44-314d-54c1-9f68-756c95a13928" :question/math-academy-id "q-18447" :question/problem "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base but no lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $64\\pi \\,{mm}^{3}.$ What is the smallest possible surface area of the cylinder?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/ce/cec96bdd2335ff5635d11c4ea6016257b35bd5450778a70ecc1f5e0156ccfe9e.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $64\\pi \\,{mm}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n64\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{64}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{64}{{r}^{2}})+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{r}+\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{128\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{128\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 2\\pi r & =\\frac{128\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 2\\pi {r}^{3} & =128\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =64 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ r & =4\\,\\text{mm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<4$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>4$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=4$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nSo the cylinder with the smallest surface area has radius $r=4\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nFinally, to find the smallest surface area, we substitute $r=4\\,\\text{mm}$ into the equation for the surface area $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(4) & =\\frac{128\\pi }{4}+\\pi (4)^{2} \\\\  & =32\\pi +16\\pi  \\\\  & =48\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible surface area of the cylinder is $S=48\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39738d1a-2b58-58e7-8b93-fae76501045c" :knowledge-point/title "Minimizing the Surface Area of a Cylinder With a Fixed Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bfc9292-582a-5130-b5d1-81e60b9f908c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "286\\pi \\,{mm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509778} {:answer/id #uuid "3aa62b7e-897f-5805-9918-dddd9fd86aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "294\\pi \\,{mm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509815} {:answer/id #uuid "b20f290b-3e64-5a30-80ba-4390b357a9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "424\\pi \\,{mm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510073} {:answer/id #uuid "d9abe97b-7da5-5c8a-875a-77732d84b70b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "324\\pi \\,{mm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510150} {:answer/id #uuid "febe6f3b-813e-5306-859b-3fbeaedf67f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "306\\pi \\,{mm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aa62b7e-897f-5805-9918-dddd9fd86aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "294\\pi \\,{mm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509815} :answer-field/id #uuid "99d98916-c136-5f7e-898c-502b466106fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "edfe2065-c6c9-560e-b435-e28eacd51b92" :question/math-academy-id "q-18451" :question/problem "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base and a lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $686\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}.$ What is the smallest surface we can get?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/ce/cec96bdd2335ff5635d11c4ea6016257b35bd5450778a70ecc1f5e0156ccfe9e.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $686\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n686\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{686}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{686}{{r}^{2}})+2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{r}+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{3} & =1\\,372\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =343 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{343} \\\\  & =7\\,\\text{mm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<7$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>7$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=7$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the smallest surface area has radius $r=7\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nFinally, to find the smallest surface area, we substitute $r=7\\,\\text{mm}$ into the equation for the surface area $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(7) & =\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{7}+2\\pi (7)^{2} \\\\  & =294\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the smallest possible surface area of the cylinder is $S=294\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8ac7b46-da1d-57f9-ab61-efa672ca8128" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing the Cost of Building a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9596c44c-aa01-5bd0-a490-4163ead1bd61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9596c44c-aa01-5bd0-a490-4163ead1bd61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510003} :answer-field/id #uuid "926323cc-4935-53fe-b3b6-46d63fc874f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f9befb55-60b4-591d-bab5-6cd215964711" :question/math-academy-id "q-315673" :question/problem "A manufacturer wishes to build a cylindrical container with a base but no lid. The container must have an exact volume of $9\\pi \\,{ft}^{3}.$\nTo make the container, the manufacturer uses two materials:\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$4.50$ per ${ft}^{2}$ for the lateral surface,\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$1.50$ per ${ft}^{2}$ for the base.\n\nWhat is the radius $r$ of the container that gives the smallest manufacturing cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/63/6393c2cbff5d727841ca78ac067ff7067114d6db8754454a30584f38e4a4ea87.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 4.50+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 1.50 \\\\  & =9\\pi rh+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $9\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n9\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =9\\pi r(\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}})+1.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81\\pi }{r}+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 3\\pi r & =\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 3\\pi {r}^{3} & =81\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=3\\,\\text{ft}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39738d1a-2b58-58e7-8b93-fae76501045c" :knowledge-point/title "Minimizing the Surface Area of a Cylinder With a Fixed Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4e759ae-9980-5d3f-9810-8593734c7be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4e759ae-9980-5d3f-9810-8593734c7be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510076} :answer-field/id #uuid "329ea85a-eb09-5964-bdf1-0a1e6d289646" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac9bf5e8-0b9b-5a4e-90d8-e2b158eca7f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-345153" :question/problem "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base but no lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $8\\pi \\,{in}^{3}.$ What is the smallest possible surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ of the cylinder? Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/7f/7fe5dad64f7c065e693ed407e0f291ec995b4823a0356e11055d20a0ca89a53b.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $8\\pi \\,{in}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n8\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{8}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{8}{{r}^{2}})+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{r}+\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{16\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{16\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 2\\pi r & =\\frac{16\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 2\\pi {r}^{3} & =16\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =8 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\ r & =2\\,\\text{in}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<2$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>2$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=2$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nFinally, to find the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}},$ we substitute $r=2\\,\\text{in}$ into the equation for the surface area $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(2) & =\\frac{16\\pi }{2}+\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =8\\pi +4\\pi  \\\\  & =12\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8ac7b46-da1d-57f9-ab61-efa672ca8128" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing the Cost of Building a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a58aaf0f-44ae-5462-9b2b-e39f22326505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a58aaf0f-44ae-5462-9b2b-e39f22326505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510035} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fd7852a-49d4-5728-a9f3-6239cb8db233" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dcb7a44b-6317-5e52-8a59-4cac3f8ca2cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-345211" :question/problem "A manufacturer wishes to build a cylindrical container with a base but no lid. The container must have an exact volume of $\\frac{27}{4}\\pi \\,{in}^{3}.$\nTo make the container, the manufacturer uses two materials:\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$6$ per ${in}^{2}$ for the lateral surface,\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$1.50$ per ${in}^{2}$ for the base.\n\nWhat is the radius $r$ of the container that gives the smallest manufacturing cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{in}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/f3/f32cf2ca384f0f1b676e77e74dd4f02484f2ba585710fee88df6951607081a42.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 6+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 1.50 \\\\  & =12\\pi rh+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $\\frac{27}{4}\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{4}\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{27}{4{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =12\\pi r(\\frac{27}{4{r}^{2}})+1.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81\\pi }{r}+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 3\\pi r & =\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 3\\pi {r}^{3} & =81\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=3\\,\\text{in}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8ac7b46-da1d-57f9-ab61-efa672ca8128" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing the Cost of Building a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217e50a3-d416-5fd8-8e38-c8d5e7424bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "217e50a3-d416-5fd8-8e38-c8d5e7424bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509757} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dc3b0ba-69af-5706-afb5-6c4ce41ddf74" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e97a779e-762a-58e2-bd98-1b5dcb7b5e80" :question/math-academy-id "q-345215" :question/problem "A manufacturer wishes to build a cylindrical container with a base but no lid. The container must have an exact volume of $\\frac{125}{3}\\pi \\,{yd}^{3}.$\nTo make the container, the manufacturer uses two materials:\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$7.50$ per ${yd}^{2}$ for the lateral surface,\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$2.50$ per ${yd}^{2}$ for the base.\n\nWhat is the radius $r$ of the container that gives the smallest manufacturing cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{yd}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/55/55de2c06ea57724513e450cbf49a0477d63cd75bac601d5d95f0ba7e366852ff.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 7.50+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 2.50 \\\\  & =15\\pi rh+2.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $\\frac{125}{3}\\pi \\,{\\text{yd}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n\\frac{125}{3}\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{125}{3{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =15\\pi r(\\frac{125}{3{r}^{2}})+2.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{625\\pi }{r}+2.5\\pi {r}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{625\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+5\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{625\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+5\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 5\\pi r & =\\frac{625\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 5\\pi {r}^{3} & =625\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =125 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{125} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<5$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>5$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=5$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=5\\,\\text{yd}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39738d1a-2b58-58e7-8b93-fae76501045c" :knowledge-point/title "Minimizing the Surface Area of a Cylinder With a Fixed Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc4354de-3fdb-5904-a007-8872deb32b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "302" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc4354de-3fdb-5904-a007-8872deb32b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "302" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510118} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9f0d32e-11c5-5d16-b8fb-323736868b33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7823091-db8e-5d0b-aebf-c2f82fe402a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-362573" :question/problem "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base and a lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $128\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}.$ What is the smallest surface area we can get? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{ft}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/bb/bb9c356aadbb184aaa49c1fa1867da82d64ef40db9048c752c2a91d20704392a.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $128\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n128\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{128}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{128}{{r}^{2}})+2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{256\\pi }{r}+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{256\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{256\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =\\frac{256\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{3} & =256\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =64 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<4$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>4$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=4$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nFinally, to find the smallest surface area, we substitute $r=4\\,\\text{ft}$ into the equation for the surface area $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(4) & =\\frac{256\\pi }{4}+2\\pi (4)^{2} \\\\  & =301.592… \\\\  & ≈302\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible surface area of the cylinder is $S=302\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8ac7b46-da1d-57f9-ab61-efa672ca8128" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing the Cost of Building a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c46be2e-d5f1-5ef8-a289-7d723f2d3602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt[3]{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509744} {:answer/id #uuid "352d0b77-d507-5fa8-9984-728f0f7b7643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509801} {:answer/id #uuid "4b86d54b-8785-555f-8b97-1ffd9f11afa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{5}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509842} {:answer/id #uuid "5db31cea-417d-52b7-9fbf-ffb47cd2996b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509883} {:answer/id #uuid "89374edb-8117-5e96-8a69-ceaff369f502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5db31cea-417d-52b7-9fbf-ffb47cd2996b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509883} :answer-field/id #uuid "34501908-abbd-572a-a01d-25d855b9ad7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ebb08e7a-2131-5a36-9f64-7c4d80a5d168" :question/math-academy-id "q-85731" :question/problem "A manufacturer wishes to build a cylindrical tank with a base but no lid. The tank must have an exact volume of $100\\pi \\,{m}^{3}.$\nTo make the tank, the manufacturer uses two materials:\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$2.5$ per ${m}^{2}$ for the lateral surface,\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$2$ per ${m}^{2}$ for the base.\n\nWhat is the radius of the tank that gives the smallest manufacturing cost?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/67/67c54d0a678e9ea9d02919abacf9b048778de19e3d0367669455439583b7a767.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 2.5+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $100\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n100\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constant equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{100}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =5\\pi r(\\frac{100}{{r}^{2}})+2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{500\\pi }{r}+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{500\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{500\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =\\frac{500\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{3} & =500\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =125 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{125} \\\\ r & =5\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<5$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>5$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=5$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=5\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fae559-f88f-50ec-a1f0-a7cfeb319d19" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Volume of a Cylinder With a Fixed Surface Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1af90d32-167e-5dde-80d0-f28ac27d5b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509740} {:answer/id #uuid "6499d744-86b6-5872-9564-ad470372e5cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509896} {:answer/id #uuid "6b30217d-8f98-5d80-acc4-6367a9a59089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509909} {:answer/id #uuid "e89826da-5376-54ed-8486-970425f19ba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510185} {:answer/id #uuid "e91eb110-0036-519e-961d-9bf3cbe43d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e91eb110-0036-519e-961d-9bf3cbe43d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510190} :answer-field/id #uuid "54b8aa91-91b7-5693-ae56-dd9da1291bd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c38e997-645f-5cbf-9057-08f6bff2619b" :question/math-academy-id "q-86214" :question/problem "![](images/ae/aefac5e84b9ea13ce039e4e2e04fda830be9ab9c17a62665ba5e5d3a7b7f6f68.png)\n\nWe need to create a cylindrical tin with no lid using $108\\pi \\,{cm}^{2}$ of a metal sheet. What is the radius $r$ of the cylinder that has the largest volume?" :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $108\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}=108\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =108\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{108\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{108-{r}^{2}}{2r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\times \\frac{108-{r}^{2}}{2r} \\\\ V(r) & =\\frac{(108r-{r}^{3})\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=\\frac{(108-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{(108-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2} & =0 \\\\ 108 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =36 \\\\ r & =6\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=6$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=\\frac{-6\\pi r}{2}=-3\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(6)<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=6$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=6\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8ac7b46-da1d-57f9-ab61-efa672ca8128" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing the Cost of Building a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abcfa87-c2d8-55b9-8b6c-f6e6dc2c2e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509713} {:answer/id #uuid "185a6029-68c4-5828-8b11-ace521807408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509734} {:answer/id #uuid "986be587-2ecf-570e-a688-8d2405f238d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510006} {:answer/id #uuid "dadc21a4-2efd-53e9-80c6-696e78b43e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510154} {:answer/id #uuid "fd82aff3-659c-5501-969a-3e3bd0a5021f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "986be587-2ecf-570e-a688-8d2405f238d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510006} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ef94707-58e0-523c-a81d-81716ae5304a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "904e690d-cf3b-5b36-80f3-f72dec8825a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-86220" :question/problem "A manufacturer wishes to build a cylindrical container with a base but no lid. The container must have an exact volume of $72\\pi \\,{ft}^{3}.$\nTo make the container, the manufacturer uses two materials:\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$1.5$ per ${ft}^{2}$ for the lateral surface,\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$0.5$ per ${ft}^{2}$ for the base.\n\nWhat is the radius of the container that gives the smallest manufacturing cost?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/95/959050ee306a96db3745f84bc7095d4e83a23ceadd819f0b749c04b20d5778d2.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 1.5+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3\\pi rh+0.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $72\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n72\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constant equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{72}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =3\\pi r(\\frac{72}{{r}^{2}})+0.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{216\\pi }{r}+0.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{216\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{216\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+\\pi r & =0 \\\\ \\pi r & =\\frac{216\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ \\pi {r}^{3} & =216\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =216 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{216} \\\\ r & =6\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<6$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>6$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=6$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=6\\,\\text{ft}.$"}]}
